WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.269
We want to continue to focus on the story

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.170
and the developments

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.939
of the detained American journalist

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:06.973
Evan Gershkovich,

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.775
who is appealing his arrest in Russia.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.543
According to Russian state media.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:11.077
The Wall Street

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.113
Journal reporter is being held in prison

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:14.581
in Russia,

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:17.317
where they hold most espionage suspects.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:18.952
American detainee Paul

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:20.920
Whelan was also held there before

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.189
he was sent to a Russian penal colony.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:25.825
Evan faces up to 20 years in prison

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.327
on these espionage charges.

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:29.529
His parents fled the Soviet Union

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.363
His colleagues say

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:31.798
he fell in love with Russia,

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:33.700
with its culture, with its language.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.602
He made Moscow his second home.

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:36.469
He also worked

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.339
to hold the Kremlin accountable.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:42.208
No longer is Russia fighting Ukraine,

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.243
but the whole West,

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:44.978
nothing that might even be taken

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:46.746
as a sign of dissent is allowed in Russia

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:47.147
right now.

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.849
It's massively early to say Russia's

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.551
you know, a dying power.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:52.852
They are worried,

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.288
extremely worried about what

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:55.989
I think

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:56.790
everybody has seen

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.458
as an increasingly erratic government

00:00:58.458 --> 00:00:59.659
in Moscow.

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:01.127
Well, friends and colleagues

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:03.029
are calling, of course, for his release,

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.597
as well as his family.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:05.832
Pyotr Souers

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.000
is a Russian affairs

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.001
reporter for The Guardian.

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.469
And here's what he writes about Evan.

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:10.970
Quote, He left a comfortable job

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.072
at The New York Times

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:12.772
to fulfill

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:14.240
his longtime dream of working

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.842
as a reporter in Moscow.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.544
He wanted to be on the ground.

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:18.778
He told me later

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.146
where the action happens.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:21.548
And Peter Sauer joins me now.

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:23.049
Thank you so much for being here.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.952
I'm so glad because,

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:26.853
you know, Evan,

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.321
we just saw videos of him

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.123
and we read his remarkable reporting

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.025
and we know his passion for Russia.

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:33.526
But can you just tell us about him,

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:34.561
the man?

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:35.795
Yeah,

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:36.930
I just want the world to know

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.865
what kind of guy avenues.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.433
And he is

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.769
the nicest, smartest, most honest guy

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.070
I've probably ever met.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.407
You know, and it's really shocking.

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:48.675
What's going on right now with him

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.710
and with his arrest.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:53.079
We don't know a lot about the conditions

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:53.680
he's under.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.682
We do know the jail he's at,

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:58.451
which is the fourth of a prison,

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.420
and that's where Wheeling was held.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.321
An official

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.789
at that prison described him

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.290
as being cheerful

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.326
and making a lot of jokes.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:07.627
Even in these conditions.

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.696
Does that sound like your friend?

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:11.264
No, that sounds exactly like Ivan.

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:13.299
You know, he had this infectious smile.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.134
He that's why

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.268
he was such a good journalist as well.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.537
He made everyone feel at ease.

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.472
Both friends and colleagues

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.307
and people he was interviewing.

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.610
And yeah, it did give me some sort of

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:25.778
gave a smile on my face

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.113
to hear that he's cracking jokes

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.081
being himself.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.550
But, of course, he should not be in jail.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.286
What why did he want to be a journalist

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:33.887
so much?

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:36.322
And why did he want to be in Russia?

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:39.025
Doing that journalism in this moment?

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:41.828
Yeah, Ivan was an all-American kid.

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.663
You know, he grew up in Jersey,

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:44.931
captained his

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.533
high school soccer team

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:47.700
but because of his parents

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.334
who were Soviet

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:48.868
emigres,

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.937
he always had this sort of fascination

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:53.072
for Russia and for the region.

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:55.408
So when he got the chance,

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:57.644
he took the job in Moscow

00:02:58.278 --> 00:02:59.779
and, you know, right away

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.848
used his understanding of Moscow

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.183
and of Russia to show the world

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:03.816
what was going on

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.285
and how the country was changing

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:07.620
You know,

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.556
this morning I was reading Nicholas.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.190
Daniel also,

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:10.557
of course,

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:13.526
is the only other journalist before Evan,

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.428
what's been since his arrest

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:18.298
on espionage charges in 1986. Right.

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:19.933
And he was held there for so long.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:20.733
Here's what he writes

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:22.702
in the Wall Street Journal this morning.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:25.271
Reporting in Russia has always been risky

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:26.139
He goes on to say,

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:27.507
We need to protect and honor

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:28.474
the bravery

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:29.008
of foreign

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:30.376
correspondents, photographers,

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.212
stringers all over the world

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:33.313
reporting in difficult

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:34.681
and dangerous circumstances

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.716
and to my fellow Russia correspondent

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.318
Evan Herskovitz.

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:40.753
Courage. Courage.

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:42.355
What do you think

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:44.390
the powerful words

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.925
Ivan will need to have a lost

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:46.826
triangle of courage?

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:48.561
I know he's strong,

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.530
but we need to keep on supporting him.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.699
You know, he took the decision.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:53.733
He thought it was his duty

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.501
to keep on reporting

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.536
out of Russia,

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:57.337
to let the world know

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.938
what was going on in the country.

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:01.474
You know, it was very important decision.

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.243
But now I think it's our duty

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:03.643
to really

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.979
let the world know what's going on with

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:06.813
its conditions.

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:08.281
His case

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.149
is, you know, it's a very,

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.251
very important case for the world.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:12.118
And when he was arrested,

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:14.053
he was reporting in Siberia, talking

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:14.721
to average

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:16.723
Russians about the war in Ukraine.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:20.159
Did he ever talk to you about the danger?

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.862
Did he feel like this was a risk for him?

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:24.998
You know, we talked every day.

00:04:24.998 --> 00:04:26.065
I mean, we talked 2 hours

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:26.833
before his arrest.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:27.900
We just exchanged text.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.468
And, of course, it's

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:29.802
something you talk about,

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.671
you know, should I still be here?

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:33.539
Is this is this the right place to be?

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:34.574
But he felt

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:35.341
you know,

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.175
no one benefits

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.610
from Russia being a black box.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.211
You know, you need

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.912
you needed to explain the story.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:40.546
You always told me.

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:41.914
I want to get the story right.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.716
He was an accredited journalist

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:44.651
from the Foreign Ministry.

00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:46.653
So you had all the right to be there.

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.454
He was just doing his job, you know?

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:49.622
Yeah. Well.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:51.824
Such a valuable and invaluable job,

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:53.493
I should say, for the world

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.862
and the reporting you do as well.

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:57.530
Pyotr, thank you very much

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:57.964
for the work

00:04:57.964 --> 00:05:00.066
you do for sharing a bit with us

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:00.900
about your friend Eva.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.002
And we are all thinking about him

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.171
and hoping that they can bring him home.

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:05.672
Thank you.

